- Fit the two original learners exactly as-is on `train_part`, then obtain out-of-fold-free holdout predictions on `valid_part` from each leg: `p_lgb`, `p_cat`.
- Instead of a raw weighted mean, build a 2-feature meta-input from the holdout predictions only: `[p_lgb, p_cat]`.
- Fit a very lightweight meta-learner on the same holdout split using a simple, monotonic model that is hard to overfit and easy to implement, such as:
  - `sklearn.linear_model.LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000)` trained on the two prediction columns, or
  - `sklearn.linear_model.RidgeClassifier`-style probability proxy if you want an even simpler linear combiner.
- Use the meta-learner only as a blender, not as a third full model: it learns the optimal combination and calibration of the two base probabilities.
- To make this more robust than plain stacking, feed the meta-learner three columns instead of two:
  - `p_lgb`
  - `p_cat`
  - `abs(p_lgb - p_cat)`
  This lets the blender learn when the models disagree and when to trust one leg more.
- Keep the base skrub DataOps pipelines intact; only add the meta layer after both learners have produced holdout predictions.
- If you want an even more stable variant with almost no extra complexity, do a two-step blend:
  - first average the two predictions,
  - then fit a single-parameter calibration on the holdout, e.g. isotonic regression or logistic calibration on the averaged score.
- Prefer the logistic meta-blend as the main plan because it is easy to code, uses only the existing holdout, and often beats a static 0.5/0.5 merge by correcting calibration and learning asymmetric weights.
- Final prediction path on the holdout should be:
  - train leg 1 and leg 2 exactly as before,
  - predict `p_lgb`, `p_cat` on `valid_part`,
  - create meta-features from those predictions,
  - fit the tiny blender on the same holdout labels,
  - predict final blended probabilities from that blender,
  - score with ROC AUC.
- This differs from the previous plan because the merge is no longer a fixed hand-tuned convex average; it is a learned calibration/stacking layer over the two model outputs, which is usually a stronger and more novel ensemble for just two complementary legs.
